WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.853
Good morning John, and thank you for your
video on Monday in which you treated us to

00:00:02.853 --> 00:00:07.770
a glimpse of the magnificently complicated
world that defines the lives of small plants

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:12.140
that happen to produce fibrous strands of
cellulose that can be converted into things…

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:13.540
sock puppet: Like me!

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:17.600
And that is mind-blowing, I mean, it’s cotton-
and you didn’t even scratch the surface

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:22.920
of how complicated the actual global cotton
industry is! The number of people, things,

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:29.609
and complexities necessary to turn, you know,
like sunlight, air, and soil into a shirt?

00:00:29.609 --> 00:00:30.609
You don’t even wanna know!

00:00:30.609 --> 00:00:35.079
It reminds me of something else you said to
me recently, you said ‘Hank, it all comes

00:00:35.079 --> 00:00:38.910
down to the fact that the truth resist simplicity.’
And you’re always saying marvelous things

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:41.559
like that, and it’s always very frustrating,
because, for me to come up with marvelous

00:00:41.559 --> 00:00:44.899
things like that I have to be like, typing
for hours and hours and hours and I’d be

00:00:44.899 --> 00:00:47.660
like, oh, that’s a good, that’s one good
thing that I came up with. But that’s why

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:50.739
you’re the professional writer and I'm the
professional…goofball. I feel like I need

00:00:50.739 --> 00:00:53.320
to do something now to prove that I'm the
professional goofball, maybe I'll just- *slaps

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:54.320
self* Ooowwww!

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:58.390
Anyway, I just love that, the truth resists
simplicity, I love it especially because,

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:02.829
It’s a simple truth talking about how rare
simple truths are. Is there a word for that?

00:01:02.829 --> 00:01:05.899
It feels like there should be a word for that.
I'm not a writer! Nerdfighters, comments,

00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:06.899
tell me if there’s a word for that.

00:01:06.899 --> 00:01:08.939
We like to think that the most complicated
things that we’re ever gonna learn are the

00:01:08.939 --> 00:01:13.060
hard sciences like math and physics and chemistry,
but really, those are the simple things. Cause

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:16.429
at least there’s definite answers. At least
at the level you’re learning about in high

00:01:16.429 --> 00:01:20.520
school, now that I think about it, chemistry
and physics, and math, there’s a lotta stuff

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:21.520
that’s not definite.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:24.429
But what’s even more complicated than that
is like, history! When your history teacher

00:01:24.429 --> 00:01:28.869
gives you a test, there’s a question that
says ‘What were the implications of the

00:01:28.869 --> 00:01:31.999
Magna Carta?’ Nobody freakin’ knows the
answer to that question! Maybe without the

00:01:31.999 --> 00:01:35.469
Magna Carta we’d all be Nazis. Maybe we’d
all be dead! Maybe we’d be fighting an evil

00:01:35.469 --> 00:01:39.499
race of genetically engineered space monsters!
Maybe we’d all be drinking pig’s milk

00:01:39.499 --> 00:01:41.570
for breakfast! Sock puppet: Probably not.

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:42.570
What?

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:44.829
Sock Puppet: There’s good reasons why we
don’t drink pig’s milk.

00:01:44.829 --> 00:01:47.259
W-like-really? Like what?

00:01:47.259 --> 00:01:48.829
Sock puppet: Well, it’s complicated.

00:01:48.829 --> 00:01:50.639
Oh, of course it’s complicated, what’s
so complicated about it?

00:01:50.639 --> 00:01:54.020
Sock puppet: Well it has to do with a lot
of different stuff, like the reproductive

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:59.340
system of the pigs, and the practice of breeding
pigs generally for meat and not for dairy-

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:03.869
Even the reason we don’t drink pigs milk
is complicated! Wow, Hank sock, I had no idea

00:02:03.869 --> 00:02:05.939
that you were such a specialist on pig…milking…practices.

00:02:05.939 --> 00:02:09.039
Sock puppet: Well, there’s a lot of stuff
you don’t know about me.

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:13.010
We love simplicity, and we want simple stories.
Like, we want there to be a homeless man with

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:17.480
a golden voice that was never recognized for
his talent until the internet found him, when

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:21.940
in actuality it’s a story of a man who went
to school and worked hard to develop his voice

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:26.890
then made a thousand bad decisions that led
to him becoming homeless, not because no one

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:30.530
recognized his talent, but because he was
addicted to alcohol and drugs.

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:37.190
We want the untainted love of Edward and Bella
to be both perfect and immortal, and it can

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:42.599
be, because it’s fake! We want simple solutions!
This intelligent looking Asian man for example

00:02:42.599 --> 00:02:47.599
thinks that maybe they can power the entire
amusement park with a hybrid drive! Even though

00:02:47.599 --> 00:02:51.879
that would violate the third law of thermodynamics!
Which apparently people at Toyota’s advertising

00:02:51.879 --> 00:02:55.329
don’t know about, uh, it’s the one where
you can’t create energy from nothing!

00:02:55.329 --> 00:02:58.790
And we wanna make simple assumptions about
the tragedy in Tucson. We want to fit it into

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:02.900
our simplistic worldview, where we can blame
the people we’re already blaming, when really,

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:08.670
it’s just a breakdown in the most complicated
system, probably in the known universe, the

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:14.579
human brain. And that same system, so thoroughly
damaged somehow, still functioning in congresswoman

00:03:14.579 --> 00:03:19.140
Giffords, proving once again that the truth.
Resists. Simplicity.

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:24.159
I think one of the principal facets of being
a mature and healthy and effective person

00:03:24.159 --> 00:03:30.061
is understanding…that you are almost always
wrong. And you might not be all the way wrong,

00:03:30.061 --> 00:03:35.849
but at least…a little bit. You’re always
a little bit wrong. We, as a species, don’t

00:03:35.849 --> 00:03:40.440
know the answer to the vast majority of questions.
And it turns out that one of the hardest parts

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:44.900
of all this, and possibly the most important
part, is accepting, that we don’t have those

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:49.370
answers. And that our simple little explanations,
are flawed. Sometimes dangerously flawed,

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:52.170
especially when we start talking about political
news analysis.

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:54.700
Thanks John, for making me think about all
that stuff, I'm gonna go play Lego Harry Potter

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:57.680
now, because at least there, I know the difference
between good and evil, John I'll see you on

00:03:57.680 --> 00:03:57.719
Friday.

